{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And Yitzchak said to Avraham his father, and he said: My father; and he said: Here am I, my son.</b> There are four utterances in this verse, and I do not know why. And they said in Bereshit Rabbah: 'ויאמר יצחק אל אברהם — Samael came to Avraham. He said to him: What is this? A son given to you at a hundred years — you go to slaughter him? He replied to him: On this condition. He went to Yitzchak. He said to him: If you are a son of a beloved one — he is going to slaughter you! He replied to him: On this condition. This is what is written: ״ויאמר יצחק אל אברהם אביו ויאמר אבי״ — why אבי twice? So that compassion upon him would be stirred, like a person who grieves over himself and says, woe, woe.' End of quote." "<b>And he said:</b> Here is the fire and the wood, and where is the lamb for the burnt-offering? And Avraham answered him: Elohim will provide Himself the lamb for a burnt-offering, my son. And then Yitzchak understood that he was the burnt-offering, and nevertheless: ״וילכו שניהם יחדו״. And on account of this, Scripture mentions a second time ״וילכו שניהם יחדו״. For in the first instance, Yitzchak did not know that he was the burnt-offering, but now that he knew, Scripture teaches you that he consented to the matter, and they went both of them together — in one intention: this one to slaughter, and this one to be slaughtered." "<b>And many will ask</b> which of them had greater strength and which of the two did the greater deed: whether Avraham, who came to slaughter his son with his own hands, or Yitzchak, who surrendered himself to be slaughtered. Some say the strength of Avraham was greater than that of Yitzchak, because one who surrenders himself to be killed — it is very much easier than the father who comes to slaughter his son with his own hands. If you do not say so, why did the Holy One, blessed be He, command Avraham to slaughter his son with his own hands? Let Him command him to kill himself, or let the command come to Yitzchak. Rather, this is certainly a proof that the strength of Avraham was greater than that of Yitzchak." "And some say the strength of Yitzchak was greater, because Avraham was commanded in this from the mouth of HaShem, whereas this one was commanded from the mouth of his father; therefore the strength of Yitzchak's faith is greater. And it appears that the opinion saying the strength of Avraham was greater is correct, for Yitzchak — even though he was not commanded from the mouth of HaShem, blessed be He — knew of his father that he was a prophet and was obligated to heed him as from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, for the word of the prophet is the word of HaShem, as it is written (Deuteronomy 18:19): ״והיה האיש אשר לא ישמע אל דברי אנכי אדרוש מעמו״. And for this reason, in this commandment the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded Avraham and not Yitzchak, to demonstrate that the strength of Avraham was greater."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:15.670175913Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}